[38;5;231m⏺[39m Now the product question at the heart of it — what "tasks they care about" actually means. This one shapes the data
  model, so I want it settled before I write anything.

  The fork: whether watching is something you [3mdo[0m or something that [3mhappens to you[0m. Explicit watching means a
  subscriptions table and a button in the UI; implicit means derived from task fields and no subscription state at all.
  Mixed means both, and it's the most work but also what most real tools converge on.

  - [1mExplicit only[0m — you click "watch" on the tasks you care about. Buys precision and zero noise; costs a cold start,
    since a brand-new task nobody has watched notifies no one, including the person it was just assigned to.
  - [1mImplicit only[0m — you're notified about tasks you created or are assigned to. Buys zero UI and sensible defaults from
    day one; costs the exact thing you said you wanted — you can't follow a task that isn't yours.
  - [1mImplicit default + explicit override[0m — auto-watch what's yours, plus a watch/unwatch button for anything else. Buys
    both; costs a subscription table that has to track "explicitly unwatched" as distinct from "never watched," which is
    a genuine source of bugs.

  Recommendation: [1mimplicit default + explicit override[0m, but with the skeleton building only the explicit half first.
  Your stated use case — "lots of tasks, I only care about a handful" — is the explicit case, so that's the part that
  needs proving. Auto-watch is a row we can insert on task creation later without changing the schema. Cheap to add, and
  building it second means the skeleton stays thin.
